      "This case addressed the question of whether the amended Section 6 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 (as amended in 2005), which conferred coparcenary rights on daughters, operated retrospectively. The central issue was whether a daughter could claim coparcenary rights in joint Hindu family property even if her father (the coparcener through whom she claims) had died before the 2005 amendment came into effect on September 9, 2005. The Supreme Court, speaking through a three-judge bench headed by Justice Arun Mishra, held that the right of a daughter under the amended Section 6 is by birth and is not dependent on whether the father coparcener was alive on September 9, 2005. The Court overruled the earlier two-judge bench decisions in Prakash v. Phulavati (2016) and Mangammal v. T.B. Raju (2018), which had held that the father must be alive on the date of the amendment for the daughter to claim coparcenary rights. The judgment gave full effect to the gender equality intended by the 2005 amendment.",
