      "This case involved a dispute over inheritance rights under the Hindu Succession Act, 1956. Chand Dhawan, the widow of the deceased, claimed rights to her husband's property. The central question was regarding the interpretation of the provisions of the Hindu Succession Act relating to the rights of a widow in her deceased husband's property and the nature of the estate she takes. The Supreme Court examined the scheme of succession under the Hindu Succession Act, particularly the provisions relating to Class I and Class II heirs under the Schedule. The Court clarified the widow's position as a Class I heir and her entitlement to an equal share along with other Class I heirs such as sons and daughters. The judgment reinforced the statutory framework for inheritance under Hindu law, moving away from the earlier concept of a limited estate for women under traditional Hindu law, which the Hindu Succession Act had abolished.",
