    fullText:
      "The case arose from a family dispute over the estate of the deceased Jawaharlal Dhawan. After his death, the question of succession to his property arose. Chand Dhawan, his widow, claimed her rightful share as a Class I heir under the Hindu Succession Act, 1956. Other family members contested her claim, raising issues about the nature and extent of her entitlement. Before the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, under traditional Hindu law as codified in various texts and as interpreted by courts, a Hindu widow had only a limited estate in her husband's property, known as the 'widow's estate.' This meant she had possession and enjoyment of the property during her lifetime, but could not alienate it, and upon her death, the property would revert to the husband's heirs rather than devolving as her own. The Hindu Succession Act, 1956, fundamentally changed this position. Section 14 of the Act converted the limited estate of a Hindu woman into an absolute estate, giving her full ownership rights over any property she possessed. The Act also placed the widow as a Class I heir in the Schedule to Section 8, giving her equal footing with sons, daughters, and the mother of the deceased. The Supreme Court examined the legislative history and purpose of the Hindu Succession Act and held that the clear intent of Parliament was to remove the disability suffered by Hindu women under the traditional law. The Court affirmed that under the Act, a Hindu widow inherits as an absolute owner and not as a limited owner. Her share is equal to that of each son, daughter, or other Class I heir. The property she inherits becomes her absolute property, which she can dispose of as she wishes during her lifetime, and upon her death, it devolves according to the rules of succession applicable to her own property. The judgment also addressed the interplay between the provisions relating to joint Hindu family property and the rights of the widow. The Court clarified that when a male Hindu dies intestate, his share in the joint family property is first ascertained by notional partition, and this share then devolves upon his heirs as per the Act. The widow is entitled to an equal share in this devolved property. This case reinforced the transformative nature of the Hindu Succession Act in establishing gender equality in Hindu inheritance law."
