      "Mary Roy, a well-known educationist and women's rights activist from Kerala (and mother of author Arundhati Roy), challenged the Travancore Christian Succession Act, 1092 (1916), and the Cochin Christian Succession Act, which governed the succession of property among Indian Christians in the erstwhile princely states of Travancore and Cochin. Under these Acts, Christian women were entitled to only one-fourth of the share of a son, or Rs. 5,000, whichever was less, from the property of the father. Mary Roy argued that these discriminatory provisions had been superseded by the Indian Succession Act, 1925, which applied uniformly to Indian Christians and gave equal rights to sons and daughters. The Supreme Court agreed, holding that the Indian Succession Act, 1925, applied to Christians in Travancore and Cochin following the integration of these princely states into the Indian Union, thereby rendering the older discriminatory statutes inoperative.",
