    fullText:
      "Mary Roy, a prominent educationist who ran the Corpus Christi School in Kottayam, Kerala, challenged the succession laws applicable to Christians in the former princely states of Travancore and Cochin. Under the Travancore Christian Succession Act, 1092 (corresponding to 1916 in the Gregorian calendar), a daughter of a Christian intestate was entitled to only one-fourth of the share of a son, or Rs. 5,000, whichever was less. Similarly, a widow was entitled to only a life interest in the husband's property, which would revert to his male heirs upon her death. These provisions were deeply discriminatory against women. Mary Roy argued before the Supreme Court that upon the integration of the princely states of Travancore and Cochin into the Indian Union and the enactment of the Part B States (Laws) Act, 1951, the Indian Succession Act, 1925, had come into force in these territories, superseding the older local succession laws. Under the Indian Succession Act, sons and daughters inherit equally from the property of an intestate. The Supreme Court, in a judgment delivered by Justices P.N. Bhagwati and R.S. Pathak, accepted Mary Roy's contention. The Court held that the Indian Succession Act, 1925, had been extended to the whole of India, including the former princely states, by virtue of the Part B States (Laws) Act, 1951. Consequently, the Travancore Christian Succession Act and the Cochin Christian Succession Act stood repealed to the extent they were inconsistent with the Indian Succession Act. This meant that Christian women in Travancore and Cochin were entitled to equal shares in intestate succession, on par with male heirs. The Court rejected the argument that the local Acts had been saved by any provision and held that the non-discriminatory provisions of the Indian Succession Act must prevail. The judgment had far-reaching consequences for the Christian community in Kerala, where a significant proportion of property was held under the rules of the old princely state succession laws. It was celebrated as a major victory for women's rights, though it also faced criticism from some segments of the Christian community who argued that it disturbed long-settled property arrangements. Mary Roy's courageous legal battle became an inspiration for women's rights movements across India and highlighted the continuing need to eliminate gender discrimination in personal law."
